-- title:
--   Transitions map Hopf kernels onto Hopf kernels, over a local PID
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative local domain which is a principal ideal ring, and let $K$ be a field that is an $R$-algebra and a fraction field of $R$, of characteristic zero. Let $p$ be a prime and let $G$ and $T$ be $p$-divisible groups over $R$ of heights $h$ and $t$: thus for each level $v$ one has commutative rings $G.\mathrm{level}\ v$ and $T.\mathrm{level}\ v$ carrying cocommutative Hopf $R$-algebra structures, finite and free as $R$-modules with $\operatorname{rank}_R = p^{vh}$ respectively $p^{vt}$, together with surjective bialgebra transition maps from level $v+1$ to level $v$ whose ring-theoretic kernel is the ideal generated by the image of the augmentation ideal under multiplication by $p^v$ in the Hopf sense. Let $\pi_v : G.\mathrm{level}\ v \to T.\mathrm{level}\ v$ be bialgebra maps over $R$ that are surjective and compatible with the transitions, i.e. $T.\mathrm{transition}_v \circ \pi_{v+1} = \pi_v \circ G.\mathrm{transition}_v$ for all $v$. Fix $v$. For a bialgebra map $\pi : A \to B$ write $\mathrm{hopfKer}(\pi)$ for the $R$-subalgebra of $A$ on which the map $(\mathrm{id}_A \otimes \pi) \circ \Delta_A : A \to A \otimes_R B$ agrees with $a \mapsto a \otimes 1$. The conclusion is that $G.\mathrm{transition}_v$ maps $\mathrm{hopfKer}(\pi_{v+1})$ onto $\mathrm{hopfKer}(\pi_v)$, that is, every element of $\mathrm{hopfKer}(\pi_v)$ is the image of an element of $\mathrm{hopfKer}(\pi_{v+1})$.
--
--   For a closed $p$-divisible subgroup $T \subseteq G$, the Hopf kernels $\mathrm{hopfKer}(\pi_v)$ are the coordinate rings of the quotients $G_v/T_v$, and the statement says that the transition maps of $G$ induce surjections between them, so that the quotients form a system of the same shape. It is the form of the surjectivity used in the construction of the quotient $p$-divisible group $G/T$ over a local principal ideal domain with fraction field of characteristic zero.
